review_text:
Me before diving into this book; 
 Library of Souls gave me so many feels before, during and after reading it. It is one of the most beautiful denouement I've ever read in my bookish life! The whole series is by far the best trilogy I've ever encountered. There were so much of plot twists that were rough and pack with too many action scenes. Riggs wrapped me into peculiardom and I just couldn't stop plunging into their world and wanting for more! 
 What I liked about the book and whole series was that Jacob's character was not rushed. Riggs developed Jacob's persona and peculiarity slowly yet stunning and crazy powerful. I also liked the fact that each of the characters had their own part to be a hero. Each situation had just the right amount of humor too that was enough to lighten things up a bit. (Way to go Addison and Enoch!) The time travel aspect and the pictures were still captivating. (Mr. Bentham had a wonderful invention btw.) I absolutely loved the main setting, kinda devilish version of Victorian London. (London reminded me so much of Harry Potter too!) The romance was well done. I still don't like that they ended up together but, oh, what can I do? 
 Me before ending; 
 Riggs' witty and imaginative thinking will leave you wondering what happens next until the very end. There was so much momentum in the book that kept me on the edge of my seat and gripped my imagination, not wanting to miss anything. Everything was just so flawless that I couldn't think of what could have done better. 
 Me after ending; 
 It was not the best book in the series but it gave such a fitting end to it. I was thinking how it would really end after defeating Caul since there were still 20 pages more. And it was just perfect to know how Jacob's life would be after peculiardom. 
 Mr. Riggs, I am ready for more. I really do. Like, no joke. Part 4 novel or even a novella would do. Just give us more of peculiardom! 
 On the whole, Miss Peregrine's Home for Peculiar Children was a great series. It was well-worth of time and money. I would recommend this to anyone depending on their maturity. The concept of peculiarity is something that everyone can embrace but the gory and creepy feel might take a while until someone truly appreciates it. I would definitely read this again and relive the story. I would even let my daughter read this when she's old enough to understand and appreciate the situation. So, if you haven't picked up Miss Peregrine's Home for Peculiar Children yet, what are you waiting for? Today is the best time, my friend. Today is the best time. 
 PS: I'M STILL SCREAMING AND CRYING A WEEK AFTER READING THIS BEAUTIFUL BOOK!!
